exercise actually chronically spikes

00:18:57.480 --> 00:19:01.240
cortisol, and chronic cortisol is the

00:18:59.560 --> 00:19:03.400
single most aging thing you can do to

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:05.120
your body. Your collagen breaks down,

00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:06.760
your skin thins out, your fat starts

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:08.520
redistributing to your face and

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:10.680
midsection, and your immune system

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:12.320
basically checks out. A study I found in

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:13.960
the Journal of Sports Sciences found

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:15.800
that overtraining syndrome can take

00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:17.960
months to recover from and during that

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:20.360
time not just your body but also your

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:22.840
mind remains under this chronic

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:24.560
psychological stress. The actual glow up

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:26.800
version of fitness is actually very

00:19:24.560 --> 00:19:29.000
boring but very relaxing. It's basically

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:30.520
simple things like taking a walk, 20

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:32.880
minutes of mat Pilates instead of an

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:34.440
hour of HIIT, maybe even a full rest

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:36.880
week where the hardest thing you do is a

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:38.960
nice shoulder and hip opening stretch.

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:40.880
And that's when the magic happens

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:43.120
because your muscles actually gets to

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:45.760
repair and you're allowing inflammation

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:47.400
to drop. Your face will depuff as a

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:48.960
result and your skin clears up. The

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:50.640
fittest people I know, the ones that

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:53.040
actually look radiant and glowing and

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:55.160
not just lean, they take breaks because

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:56.520
it's strategic and it's not a sign that

00:19:55.160 --> 00:19:58.920
they're lazy. They're absolutely not

00:19:56.520 --> 00:20:02.000
lazy. The best athletes take the

00:19:58.920 --> 00:20:03.840
recovery extremely seriously. So, take a

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:06.080
break if you need to. Point number 10 is

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:08.040
cutting people off and yeah, I mean even

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:09.840
the ones you love if they're no good for

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:11.200
you. If you've done this before, you

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:13.000
already know what I'm saying and why I

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:14.800
mentioned this. There's a version of

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:16.600
cutting people off that's reactive and

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:18.320
avoidant like you ghost someone. That's

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:19.760
not what I'm talking about. I'm talking

00:20:18.320 --> 00:20:21.520
about when you already talked it out,

00:20:19.760 --> 00:20:24.000
you set boundaries, you gave it some

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:25.680
time, and this relationship is still

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:27.680
draining you. Every time you leave their

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:30.240
presence, you feel dull instead of

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:31.920
energized. Your nervous system literally

00:20:30.240 --> 00:20:34.560
mirrors the people you spent the most

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:36.920
time with. It's neuroscience. Vittorio

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:38.600
Gallese and Giacomo Rizzolatti

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:40.840
discovered mirror neurons at the

00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:43.240
University of Parma in the 1990s and

00:20:40.840 --> 00:20:45.480
what they found is that your brain fires

00:20:43.240 --> 00:20:47.760
in response to the emotional states of

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:50.040
people around you as though you're also

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:51.880
experiencing the same emotions yourself.

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:54.440
If you're consistently around someone

00:20:51.880 --> 00:20:56.440
who is anxious, critical, chaotic, your

00:20:54.440 --> 00:20:58.880
body is also absorbing that like a

00:20:56.440 --> 00:21:00.600
sponge. When I cut off my old stressful

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:03.560
job where people were bullying me,

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:05.080
within literally days, the HR lady who

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:06.880
saw me after because I had to return my

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:09.000
work laptop, she was telling me that I

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:11.320
looked so much better and I was like, I

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:13.160
know, and it's because you fired me. And

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:15.200
I felt so good not being in that

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:16.640
environment. If there's no way you can

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:18.760
get out of the environment that stresses

00:21:16.640 --> 00:21:20.720
you out, then try to at least not be in

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:22.520
the same room as much as you can for the

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:24.280
sake of your sanity and also your glow

00:21:22.520 --> 00:21:26.200
up because your relationships are

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:28.400
absolutely a part of your glow up, too.

00:21:26.200 --> 00:21:30.880
So, don't feel bad if you're lonely

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:32.600
because I'd rather be alone all the time

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:34.480
than to be with a group of people who

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:36.960
have toxic vibes. I would rather be a

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:38.040
monk or a nun than to deal with people

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:40.640
that I don't want to deal with. And

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:42.480
finally, point number 11, being angry

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:44.240
and then processing it and moving it

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:46.280
somewhere it can actually go. I'm

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:47.920
talking about healthy strategies. So, no

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:49.320
to throwing things across your room or

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:50.520
punishing someone who had nothing to do

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:52.040
with it unless you're cool with throwing

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:53.960
stuff around your room, but unless you

00:21:52.040 --> 00:21:55.880
go to like an ax-throwing place and you

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:57.920
unleashing your anger, I mean, that can

00:21:55.880 --> 00:22:00.080
be fun, too, but don't just swallow that

00:21:57.920 --> 00:22:02.040
anger. That suppression has to go

00:22:00.080 --> 00:22:04.080
somewhere. If it's not channeled out of

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:06.600
your body in a healthy way, then it's

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:08.840
being held in your body as inflammation

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:10.080
and stress. So, I want to tell you what

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:11.640
I actually do because it might sound

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:13.920
silly, but it really works. I have

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:15.920
stress balls everywhere on my desk, on

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:17.520
my nightstand, in my back, like stuff

00:22:15.920 --> 00:22:19.040
that I fidget with and I fidget with it

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:21.240
all the time. Like when I feel that

00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:22.960
energy start to build up, I need a place

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:24.280
for it to go that isn't my muscles

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:26.480
clenching down or, you know, I don't

00:22:24.280 --> 00:22:28.960
want it to go to my face. So, squeezing

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:30.800
something like this fidget toy activates

00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:33.120
the nerves in your hands that send

00:22:30.800 --> 00:22:34.720
calming signals to your limbic system.

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:36.880
There's a whole body of research on

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:38.360
nervous system regulation and it works

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:41.040
because you're giving your fight or

00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:43.280
flight energy a physical outlet instead

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:45.160
of trapping it in your muscles. Other

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:47.760
people channel it differently like going

00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:49.720
on a hot sprint or doing heavy deadlifts

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:51.880
or screaming into a pillow, whatever it

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:54.080
takes, right? The tool doesn't matter as

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:56.600
much as having one because anger without

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:58.200
an outlet just becomes tension and

00:22:56.600 --> 00:23:00.160
sometimes tension might just be the

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:01.440
thing standing between you and your glow

00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:03.440
up. As long as you channel it in a

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:05.480
healthy way without hurting other people

00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:07.280
or yourself, then it's great. I

00:23:05.480 --> 00:23:09.280
personally love to channel it out in my

00:23:07.280 --> 00:23:11.320
personal work, too. I love talking about

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:12.520
being angry. Sometimes, like, I don't

00:23:11.320 --> 00:23:13.920
even know if it's because I'm also

00:23:12.520 --> 00:23:16.440
neurodivergent, but I have this

00:23:13.920 --> 00:23:19.080
inexplicable rage that I need to channel

00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:20.920
out, too. But your body truly deserves

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:22.680
better than to hold on to something that

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:24.120
doesn't serve you. So, look, you don't

00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:25.600
have to do everything on this list, so

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:26.800
